As said before: a struct -> CSV writer is all that is necessary for
that.

(based on the idea of struct to XML writer).

For example, make a program that reads in:

struct mystructure{
int blah;
int blah2;
bool a
char *mystring;
};

And it makes the following code (akin to makedefs):

void
mystructurewriter(s, fd)
struct mystructure* s;
int fd;
{
fputs( Itoa(s->blah), fd); //where itoa returns a preallocated memory,
no need to free.
fputs( Itoa(s->blah2), fd); // fputs makes newline after all of this
fputs( bool(s->a), fd);
fputs( mystring, fd);
}

void
mystructurereader(s, fd)
etc. etc.

Basically; every struct read in by this program (done at the time of
makedefs) will make a CSV of the file. Well, not a CSV, this is
separated
by newlines only, but it could be easily modified. Automatic
maintainance,
and such a macro writer/reader would be eaiser to make than an
XML equivalent.

